# How ReSTIR Lights a Room of Many Lights {#video subtitle="Bitterli et al. 2020, spatiotemporal reservoir resampling, implemented from the paper" date=2026-11-12 fps=30 width=1920 height=1080}

## The paper and its problem {#c1 number=1 target=235 summary="The paper, the problem it solves, how it solves it, and its contributions." sources="problem, approach, reservoir-intro, pdfs-not-colors, biased-mode, first-vertex"}

### The paper {#c1-paper kind=2d diagram=paper}

${cue "key" {' {callout "problem"}}}This video is about a paper by Benedikt Bitterli, Chris Wyman, Matt Pharr, Peter Shirley, Aaron Lefohn, and Wojciech Jarosz.

It is called Spatiotemporal reservoir resampling for real-time ray tracing with dynamic direct lighting.

It was published in ACM Transactions on Graphics in 2020.

### Sixty-four moving lights {#c1-problem kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoRoom panelHidden {append baseRoom {append roomCamera {noReuse 1.0}}}}}
${cue "key" floatRoom}

${overText {' {lightPhaseRange 0.0 0.06}} "This room is lit by sixty-four small lights, and every one of them moves"}.

${overText {' {lightPhaseRange 0.06 0.12}} "To light one pixel, a renderer must add up what every light sends to it"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "first-vertex"}}}${overText {' {lightPhaseRange 0.12 0.18}} "A shadow ray tells whether a light reaches a pixel, and in real time a pixel can afford only a few"}.

So each pixel has to guess which lights are worth testing.

${overText {' {lightPhaseRange 0.18 0.24}} "Here each pixel picks one light at random, favouring the brighter ones"}.

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraDistance 4.2 sentence}}}Most guesses land on a light that adds little to that pixel, so the image is full of noise.

${cue "key" {' {callout "problem"}}}${overText {' {lightPhaseRange 0.24 0.3}} "The paper asks how each pixel can find the lights that matter to it while they move, and still run in real time"}.

### The answer, in three steps {#c1-solution kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoRoom panelHidden {append baseRoom {append roomCamera {noReuse 1.0}}}}}
${cue "key" floatRoom}

${cue "key" {' {callout "approach"}}}${overText {' {restirCandidatesRange 1.0 32.0}} "The answer starts with many cheap guesses for each pixel"}.

Ignoring shadows, a pixel can estimate cheaply how much light each guess would bring.

It keeps one guess, chosen in proportion to that estimate.

${overText {' {restirSpatialPassesRange 0.0 2.0}} "Then each pixel borrows the guesses its neighbours kept"}.

${overText {' {restirTemporalRange 0.0 1.0}} "It also borrows the guess the same point kept in the previous frame"}.

Nearby pixels and nearby moments usually need the same lights, so their guesses help each other.

${cue "key" {' {callout "reservoir-intro"}}}${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 28.0 sentence}}}Only the guesses a pixel keeps need shadow rays, so the rays per pixel stay few.

${cue "key" {' {callout "pdfs-not-colors"}}}Unlike a denoiser, the method shares what each pixel learned about which lights to pick, not the colours of pixels.

That is what makes an unbiased version possible, one that is correct on average.

${cue "key" {' {callout "biased-mode"}}}${overText {' {restirUnbiasedRange 0.0 1.0}} "It also offers a biased version, which reaches the same quality faster but darkens the edges of objects a little"}.

### Four contributions {#c1-contributions kind=2d diagram=contributions}

${cue "key" {' {callout "name"}}}The paper calls its method ReSTIR, short for reservoir-based spatiotemporal importance resampling.

This video groups the paper's ideas into four contributions.

### Streaming {#c1-c-stream kind=2d diagram=contributions focus=STREAM}

${cue "key" {' {callout "approach"}}}First, a pixel keeps a tiny record called a reservoir, instead of a list of its guesses.

### Reuse {#c1-c-reuse kind=2d diagram=contributions focus=REUSE}

Second, reservoirs can be combined across neighbouring pixels and across frames.

### No bias {#c1-c-unbiased kind=2d diagram=contributions focus=UNBIASED}

${cue "key" {' {callout "pdfs-not-colors"}}}Third, it shows where that reuse introduces bias, and gives a weight that removes it.

### On the GPU {#c1-c-gpu kind=2d diagram=contributions focus=GPU}

${cue "key" {' {callout "biased-mode"}}}Fourth, it runs on the GPU in real time, in a biased and an unbiased form.

### The passes of a frame {#c1-map kind=2d diagram=alg5 focus="RIS, VIS, TEMP, SPAT, SHADE"}

Every frame, the method runs as a chain of GPU passes, each one a step over all the pixels.

Each pixel draws its guesses, tests the one it keeps, borrows from the previous frame and from its neighbours, and is then shaded.

### Last frame feeds the next {#c1-map-loop kind=2d diagram=alg5 focus="PREV, TEMP"}

Each frame's reservoirs are kept for the next frame, which closes a loop.

### What this story teaches {#c1-teach kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoRoom panelHidden {append baseRoom roomCamera}}}
${cue "key" floatRoom}

${overText {' {lightPhaseRange 0.0 0.08}} "By the end, you will be able to explain each pass, and why the result stays correct"}.

${overText {' {lightPhaseRange 0.08 0.16}} "On the way, the video teaches the two ideas the method is built from"}.

They are resampled importance sampling and weighted reservoir sampling.

${overText {' {lightPhaseRange 0.16 0.24}} "It assumes you know Monte Carlo integration with importance sampling, and how a physically based renderer shades a surface"}.

${overText {' {lightPhaseRange 0.24 0.32}} "Every demonstration is rendered by the lab, the program that made this video, with the paper's algorithms written from its text"}.

### Where to read it {#c1-sources kind=2d sources="problem, approach, reservoir-intro, pdfs-not-colors, biased-mode, first-vertex"}

The paper's introduction covers this chapter, on its first two pages.

## Related work {#c2 number=2 target=285 summary="What ReSTIR replaces, and the two ideas it is built from: resampled importance sampling and weighted reservoir sampling." sources="many-lights, hierarchies, tiled, path-reuse, denoisers, eq3, eq4, eq5, eq6, ris-unbiased, ris-converges, wrs, eq9, alg2"}

### Other ways to light many lights {#c2-others kind=2d diagram=related focus=OTHERS}

The paper discusses related work after its method, in section seven, but it helps to meet it first.

### Light trees {#c2-many kind=2d diagram=related focus=MANY}

${cue "key" {' {callout "many-lights"}}}Many-light methods sort lights by importance, often into a tree that can be sampled quickly.

${cue "key" {' {callout "hierarchies"}}}A tree has to be maintained as its lights move, and a real-time frame has little time for that.

### Tiles {#c2-tiled kind=2d diagram=related focus=TILED}

${cue "key" {' {callout "tiled"}}}Tiled shading keeps a short list of lights for each tile of the screen.

To keep the lists short, each light's reach is typically limited to an area, which biases the image.

### Shared paths {#c2-paths kind=2d diagram=related focus=PATHS}

${cue "key" {' {callout "path-reuse"}}}Path reuse shares the paths light takes between pixels, but traces a new shadow ray each time a path is reused.

### Denoisers {#c2-denoise kind=2d diagram=related focus=DENOISE}

${cue "key" {' {callout "denoisers"}}}Denoisers blur noisy colours after rendering, and ReSTIR can give them better samples to start from.

### Two foundations {#c2-foundations kind=2d diagram=related focus="FOUNDATIONS, RESTIR"}

ReSTIR itself is built from two older ideas, resampled importance sampling and weighted reservoir sampling.

### Importance sampling, recalled {#c2-is kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoRis panelHidden {append baseRoom {append pickCamera {append {noReuse 1.0} {' {restirSeed 5.0} {risStep 1.0}}}}}}}
${cue "key" floatPick}

${cue "key" {' {callout "eq3"}}}Both ideas start from importance sampling.

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 28.0 sentence}}}To estimate the light at a point P, we draw one light sample.

Its estimate is ${formulaSaid f"{\frac{f(x)}{p(x)}}" "its contribution f, divided by p, the probability it was drawn with"
    {'}}.

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 22.0 sentence}}}The more closely that probability follows each sample's contribution, the less noise remains.

${cue "key" {' {callout "eq4"}}}${cue "key" {' {glide cameraDistance 3.3 sentence}}}Multiple importance sampling combines several such strategies with weights that sum to one, an idea the paper returns to when it removes bias.

### What we would like to sample {#c2-ris-question kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoRis panelHidden {append baseRoom {append pickCamera {append {noReuse 1.0} {' {restirSeed 5.0} {risStep 1.0}}}}}}}
${cue "key" floatPick}

${cue "key" {' {callout "eq5"}}}${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 30.0 sentence}}}The ideal is to draw each light sample in proportion to the light it would bring to P.

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 24.0 sentence}}}That depends on the light's emission, on P's surface, and on the angle and distance between them.

The paper calls that ideal shape the target, written p-hat.

${overText {' {restirSeedRange 5.0 9.0}} "Nobody can draw samples in exactly that proportion"}.

${overText {' {restirSeedRange 9.0 13.0}} "The simplest practical choice, which this lab starts from, draws lights in proportion to their power, ignoring P entirely"}.

### Resampled importance sampling {#c2-ris kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoRis panelValues {append baseRoom {append pickCamera {append {noReuse 8.0} {' {restirSeed 5.0} {risStep 8.0}}}}}}}
${cue "key" floatPick}

${cue "key" {' {callout "eq5"}}}Resampled importance sampling, or RIS, makes a cheap guess many times, then keeps one.

${overText {' {risStepRange 0.0 8.0}} "Here P draws eight candidates"}.

Each one is a light picked by power, and a point on that light.

Each candidate gets a weight, ${formulaSaid f"{w = \frac{\hat{p}(x)}{p(x)}}" "its target divided by the probability it was drawn with"
    {'}}.

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 30.0 sentence}}}A candidate that would bring more light is drawn as a brighter line from P.

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 24.0 sentence}}}P keeps one candidate, chosen with a probability in proportion to its weight.

${cue "key" {' {callout "eq6"}}}The kept sample is then shaded as if it had been drawn in proportion to the target.

It was not, so the result is multiplied by a correction, the average of all the weights.

${cue "key" {' {callout "ris-unbiased"}}}With that correction, RIS is unbiased for any number of candidates.

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraDistance 3.2 sentence}}}The panel folds that correction and one over the kept sample's target into a single number, W.

The pixel's light is then the kept sample's contribution times W.

### More candidates, less noise {#c2-ris-more kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoReservoirs panelHidden {append baseRoom {append roomCamera {noReuse 1.0}}}}}
${cue "key" floatRoom}

${cue "key" {' {callout "ris-converges"}}}${overText {' {restirCandidatesRange 1.0 8.0}} "With more candidates, the kept sample follows the target more closely"}.

${overText {' {restirCandidatesRange 8.0 32.0}} "The noise in the room falls as the count rises to thirty-two"}.

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 26.0 sentence}}}The cost rises with it, because every candidate has to be weighted.

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 18.0 sentence}}}The target also ignores shadows, so a kept light may still be hidden from P.

This is exactly the first pass of ReSTIR, run for every pixel.

### Keeping one from a stream {#c2-wrs kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoRis panelCode {append baseRoom {append pickCamera {append {noReuse 8.0} {'
    listingRis
    {highlightCode "ris" {' "candidate"}}
    {restirSeed 5.0}
    {risStep 0.0}}}}}}}
${cue "key" floatPick}

The second foundation, weighted reservoir sampling, solves a problem of storage.

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 28.0 sentence}}}As described, RIS keeps every candidate until it chooses, and a GPU cannot afford a list for every pixel.

${cue "key" {' {callout "wrs"}}}It chooses one item from a stream, a sequence it sees one item at a time, and only once.

${cue "key" {' {callout "alg2"}}}Its reservoir holds just the kept item, the running sum of weights, and the count of items seen.

${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "ris" {' "update"}} {risStep 1.0}}}The first candidate arrives, and the reservoir keeps it.

${cue "key" {' {callout "eq9"}}}${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "ris" {' "keep"}}}}Each later candidate replaces the kept one with a probability equal to its weight divided by the new sum.

${overText {' {risStepRange 1.0 8.0}} "One by one, the candidates stream through the reservoir"}.

${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "ris" {' "finish"}}}}At the end, each candidate has been kept in proportion to its weight, exactly as RIS needs.

${cue "key" {' {callout "combine"}}}${cue "key" {' {glide cameraDistance 3.3 sentence}}}Two reservoirs can also be merged as if one stream had passed through both, which is what lets pixels share their work.

### Where to read it {#c2-sources kind=2d sources="many-lights, hierarchies, tiled, path-reuse, denoisers, eq3, eq4, eq5, eq6, ris-unbiased, ris-converges, wrs, eq9, alg2"}

The related work is in section seven, and the two foundations are in section two.

## Streaming RIS {#c3 number=3 target=105 summary="RIS through one reservoir per pixel: what it stores, the paper's choices, and what it costs." sources="alg3, storage, candidates-param, alias, target-param, streaming-cost"}

### One reservoir per pixel {#c3-alg3 kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoRis panelCode {append baseRoom {append pickCamera {append {noReuse 8.0} {'
    listingRis
    {highlightCode "ris" {' "candidate"}}
    {restirSeed 5.0}
    {risStep 8.0}}}}}}}
${cue "key" floatPick}

${cue "key" {' {callout "alg3"}}}Combined, the two ideas become streaming RIS, the first pass of every frame.

${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "ris" {' "weight"}}}}${overText {' {risStepRange 0.0 8.0}} "Each pixel streams its candidates through one reservoir, and keeps only the reservoir"}.

${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "ris" {' "finish"}}}}When the stream ends, the pixel turns the sum of weights into W, ${formulaSaid f"{W = \frac{\sum w}{M\, \hat{p}(y)}}" "the sum divided by the count M and by the kept sample's target"
    {'}}.

${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "ris" {' "shade"}}}}${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 30.0 sentence}}}The pixel is shaded with what the kept sample brings, multiplied by W.

### What a reservoir holds {#c3-store kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoReservoirs panelHidden {append baseRoom {append roomCamera {append {noReuse 32.0} {' {restirView 1.0}}}}}}}
${cue "key" floatRoom}

${cue "key" {' {callout "storage"}}}A reservoir needs only three things, the sample y, the count M, and the weight W.

Here each pixel is coloured by the light its sample is on.

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 26.0 sentence}}}Neighbouring pixels on one surface often keep the same lights, because those lights matter to all of them.

${cue "key" {' {restirView 3.0}}}This view shows W, which is larger where the kept sample was unlikely to be drawn, so that it counts for more.

${cue "key" {' {restirView 2.0}}}And here M is thirty-two in every lit pixel, the number of candidates each one drew.

### The paper's choices {#c3-params kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoReservoirs panelHidden {append baseRoom {append roomCamera {noReuse 32.0}}}}}
${cue "key" floatRoom}

${cue "key" {' {callout "candidates-param"}}}The paper uses thirty-two candidates per pixel.

${cue "key" {' {callout "alias"}}}${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 24.0 sentence}}}It picks an emissive triangle by its power from an alias table, then a uniform point on that triangle.

${overText {' {lightPhaseRange 0.0 0.1}} "The lab's lights are spheres, picked by power from a running sum with a binary search, which gives the same distribution"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "target-param"}}}As in RIS, the target is the light a sample would bring if nothing were in the way.

${cue "key" {' {callout "streaming-cost"}}}${overText {' {restirCandidatesRange 32.0 64.0}} "Storage is now constant, but the work still grows with every candidate"}.

So instead of drawing more, the next steps reuse candidates already drawn.

### Where to read it {#c3-sources kind=2d sources="alg3, storage, candidates-param, alias, target-param, streaming-cost"}

Streaming RIS is in section three point one, and the paper's choices are in section five.

## Spatial reuse {#c4 number=4 target=140 summary="Combining reservoirs, the neighbours each pixel combines, the rounds that multiply its candidates, and the test that rejects some." sources="combine, reweight, alg4, neighbors-param, spatial, spatial-passes, geometry-test"}

### Combining two reservoirs {#c4-combine kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoSpatial panelCode {append baseRoom {append pickCamera {'
    listingCombine
    {highlightCode "combine" {' "update"}}
    {restirTemporal 0.0}
    {restirSpatialPasses 1.0}
    {restirSeed 5.0}}}}}}
${cue "key" floatPick}

Each pixel now holds one reservoir, and its neighbours hold theirs.

${cue "key" {' {callout "combine"}}}${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 28.0 sentence}}}Combining two reservoirs treats each kept sample as a new candidate.

${cue "key" {' {callout "reweight"}}}Its weight is ${formulaSaid f"{\hat{p}_P(y) \cdot W \cdot M}" "the target at P, times the reservoir's W, times its M"
    {'}}.

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 22.0 sentence}}}The target is evaluated at P, because a sample that suits a neighbour may not suit P.

${cue "key" {' {callout "alg4"}}}${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "combine" {' "count"}}}}The combined reservoir stands for every candidate behind both, so their counts add up.

### Neighbours in a disc {#c4-neighbours kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoSpatial panelValues {append baseRoom {append pickCamera {'
    {restirTemporal 0.0}
    {restirSpatialPasses 1.0}
    {restirNeighbours 1.0}
    {restirSeed 5.0}}}}}}
${cue "key" floatPick}

${cue "key" {' {callout "neighbors-param"}}}${overText {' {restirNeighboursRange 1.0 5.0}} "Spatial reuse runs in rounds, and in each round P picks k neighbours at random within thirty pixels"}.

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 30.0 sentence}}}The paper takes them from a low-discrepancy sequence, because deterministic neighbours caused distracting artifacts.

The lab uses the R2 sequence for it.

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 24.0 sentence}}}Each line here runs from P to a neighbour, and on to the sample that neighbour's reservoir holds.

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraDistance 3.3 sentence}}}Combining them, P's count M grows by the candidates of every neighbour it takes.

### Rounds multiply the candidates {#c4-rounds kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoReservoirs panelHidden {append baseRoom {append roomCamera {'
    {restirTemporal 0.0}
    {restirSpatialPasses 0.0}
    {restirStage 3.0}}}}}}
${cue "key" floatRoom}

${cue "key" {' {callout "spatial"}}}${overText {' {restirSpatialPassesRange 0.0 1.0}} "One round already removes much of the noise"}.

${overText {' {restirSpatialPassesRange 1.0 2.0}} "A second round combines the reservoirs the first one made, so the candidates multiply"}.

Each round multiplies the candidates again, so after n rounds, with distinct neighbours, a pixel can stand for ${formulaSaid f"{k^n M}" "k to the power n, times M"
    {'}} candidates.

${cue "key" {' {restirView 2.0}}}This view shows M, which is much larger than the thirty-two each pixel drew.

${cue "key" {' {restirView 0.0}}}${overText {' {restirSpatialPassesRange 2.0 4.0}} "The gain fades once the rounds have gathered the nearby candidates, since later rounds find the same ones again"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "spatial-passes"}}}${overText {' {restirSpatialPassesRange 4.0 2.0}} "The paper uses two rounds, or one in its unbiased version"}.

### Neighbours across an edge {#c4-test kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoSpatial panelValues {append baseRoom {append pickCamera {'
    {restirTemporal 0.0}
    {restirSpatialPasses 1.0}
    {restirNeighbours 12.0}
    {restirSeed 5.0}
    {pickU 0.56}
    {pickV 0.64}}}}}}
${cue "key" floatPick}

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 28.0 sentence}}}A neighbour across the edge of an object sees different light from P.

${cue "key" {' {callout "geometry-test"}}}In its biased algorithm, the paper skips a neighbour whose depth differs by more than ten percent, or whose normal differs by more than twenty-five degrees.

The lab applies the same test to the previous frame's pixel too.

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 22.0 sentence}}}Here the neighbours it skips are drawn in red.

${overText {' {restirGeometryTestRange 1.0 0.0}} "Without this test, those neighbours would pass on samples that suit P poorly"}.

### Where to read it {#c4-sources kind=2d sources="combine, reweight, alg4, neighbors-param, spatial, spatial-passes, geometry-test"}

Spatial reuse is in section three point two, and its parameters in section five.

## Temporal and visibility reuse {#c5 number=5 target=123 summary="Reusing the frame before, the clamp on its count, the moving lights, and the shadow ray before reuse." sources="temporal, temporal-pick, clamp, visibility, alg5"}

### The frame before {#c5-temporal kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoReuse panelValues {append baseRoom {append pickCamera {' {restirSpatialPasses 0.0}}}}}}
${cue "key" floatPick}

Neighbours share across the screen, and the previous frame offers still more.

${cue "key" {' {callout "temporal"}}}After each frame, every pixel's final reservoir, the one it was shaded with, is kept.

${cue "key" {' {callout "temporal-pick"}}}${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 30.0 sentence}}}In the next frame, each pixel finds where its point was in the previous frame, and combines that pixel's reservoir with its own.

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 22.0 sentence}}}The kept reservoir already holds the frames before it, so every past frame feeds the present one.

${cue "key" {' {restirView 2.0}}}${cue "key" {' {glide cameraDistance 3.2 sentence}}}Each combination adds the counts, so M keeps climbing from frame to frame.

${cue "key" {' {callout "clamp"}}}Left alone, it would grow without limit, and old samples would outweigh new ones.

${overText {' {restirTemporalClampRange 20.0 2.0}} "The paper therefore clamps the previous frame's M to twenty times this frame's"}.

### Lights on the move {#c5-dynamic kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoReuse panelValues {append baseRoom {append roomCamera {' {restirSpatialPasses 0.0}}}}}}
${cue "key" floatRoom}

${overText {' {lightPhaseRange 0.0 0.1}} "The lights keep moving throughout"}.

${overText {' {lightPhaseRange 0.1 0.2}} "In the lab, a sample is a light plus a point on it, so it follows its light as the light moves"}.

${overText {' {lightPhaseRange 0.2 0.3}} "Its target is evaluated again at the light's new position whenever it is combined"}.

### A shadow ray before reuse {#c5-visibility kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoReuse panelValues {append baseRoom {append pickCamera {' {restirSpatialPasses 0.0} {restirView 4.0} {restirStage 1.0}}}}}}
${cue "key" floatPick}

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 28.0 sentence}}}The target ignores shadows, so a kept sample may be hidden from its pixel.

${cue "key" {' {callout "visibility"}}}Before any reuse, each pixel traces one shadow ray to the sample it kept.

${cue "key" {' {callout "alg5"}}}${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 22.0 sentence}}}If the ray is blocked, W becomes zero, but the reservoir keeps its M, the candidates it stands for.

Here green pixels kept a visible sample, and red pixels a hidden one.

${cue "key" {' {restirView 0.0} {restirStage 3.0} {restirSpatialPasses 2.0}}}A hidden sample then cannot spread to neighbouring pixels.

${overText {' {restirVisibilityReuseRange 1.0 0.0}} "Without the test, as the lab shows here, shadows fill with noise, because neighbours pass on lights they cannot see"}.

### The whole frame {#c5-alg5 kind=2d diagram=alg5 focus="RIS, VIS, TEMP, SPAT, SHADE, PREV"}

${cue "key" {' {callout "alg5"}}}That completes one frame of the paper's algorithm.

The candidates come first, then visibility, the previous frame, the neighbours, and finally the shading.

### Where to read it {#c5-sources kind=2d sources="temporal, temporal-pick, clamp, visibility, alg5"}

Temporal and visibility reuse are in section three point two, and the clamp in section five.

## Bias, and removing it {#c6 number=6 target=176 summary="Why reuse biases RIS, the paper's one-dimensional example, the weights that remove the bias, and the unbiased combination." sources="bias-source, eq12, weight-meaning, eq19, fig7, eq20, naive-m, eq22, talbot-cost, alg6, approx-pdf, unbiased-cost"}

### Every pixel has its own target {#c6-source kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoSpatial panelValues {append baseRoom {append pickCamera {' {restirTemporal 0.0} {restirSpatialPasses 1.0} {restirSeed 5.0}}}}}}
${cue "key" floatPick}

The reuse so far has one flaw.

${cue "key" {' {callout "bias-source"}}}${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 28.0 sentence}}}Every pixel has its own target, because its surface faces its own way and sees its own lights.

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 22.0 sentence}}}A sample from a neighbour was chosen for the neighbour's target, not for P's.

${cue "key" {' {callout "eq12"}}}Recall that W stands in for one over the probability of the kept sample.

${cue "key" {' {callout "weight-meaning"}}}On average, W must equal that exactly, or the image comes out too bright or too dark.

### The paper's example in one dimension {#c6-1d kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoBias panelHidden {append baseRoom {append roomCamera {' {biasWeight 0.0} {biasCandidates 2.0} {biasNearZero 0.0}}}}}}
${cue "key" floatRoom}

${cue "key" {' {callout "fig7"}}}The paper shows the problem in one dimension.

Here the target is ${formulaSaid f"{\hat{p}(x) = 2 - 2x}" "a ramp that falls from two to zero"
    {'}}.

Half the candidates come from a flat distribution, and half from a step that covers only the left half.

${overText {' {biasCandidatesRange 2.0 4.0}} "The thick line is the true inverse probability of RIS's output, measured over many runs"}.

${overText {' {biasCandidatesRange 4.0 10.0}} "The thin line is the average of W at each place the output lands"}.

On the right half, the step can never produce a sample, yet W still divides by every candidate, the step's included.

${cue "key" {' {callout "eq19"}}}${overText {' {biasCandidatesRange 10.0 20.0}} "So W is too small there, by half, and an image would come out too dark"}.

${cue "key" {' {callout "approx-pdf"}}}In ReSTIR, each reservoir a pixel combines is such a source of candidates.

A neighbour whose surface faces away, or whose shadow hides a light, cannot produce some samples P can use.

### Counting only those that could {#c6-fix kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoBias panelHidden {append baseRoom {append roomCamera {' {biasWeight 0.0} {biasCandidates 4.0} {biasNearZero 0.0}}}}}}
${cue "key" floatRoom}

${cue "key" {' {callout "eq20"}}}The fix is to divide only by the candidates that could have produced the sample.

${cue "key" {' {callout "naive-m"}}}${overText {' {biasWeightRange 0.0 1.0}} "With that count, the two lines agree"}.

${overText {' {biasNearZeroRange 0.0 1.0}} "But when the step is close to zero rather than exactly zero, this count makes W very noisy"}.

A candidate drawn from the step there has a tiny probability, and so a huge weight.

${cue "key" {' {callout "eq22"}}}${overText {' {biasWeightRange 1.0 2.0}} "The paper's better weight is the balance heuristic, from multiple importance sampling"}.

It weights each candidate by how likely its own distribution was to produce the sample, compared with all of them.

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 26.0 sentence}}}It agrees with the truth, and it stays stable.

${cue "key" {' {callout "talbot-cost"}}}When every candidate has its own distribution, Talbot's original method evaluates every probability for every candidate, so its cost grows with the square of their number.

### The unbiased combination {#c6-alg6 kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoMethods panelCode {append baseRoom {append roomCamera {'
    listingCombine
    {highlightCode "combine" {' "unbiased"}}
    {restirUnbiased 0.0}}}}}}
${cue "key" floatRoom}

${cue "key" {' {callout "alg6"}}}${cue "key" {' {highlightCode "combine" {' "z"}}}}In the room, the unbiased combination uses the simple count from the paper's algorithm six.

It counts only the sources, P and its neighbours, whose target at the sample is not zero.

The paper says a version with the balance heuristic works the same way.

${cue "key" {' {callout "unbiased-cost"}}}With visibility reuse, that check needs one extra shadow ray per neighbour.

${cue "key" {' {callout "biased-mode"}}}${overText {' {restirUnbiasedRange 0.0 1.0}} "The biased version skips that cost, and darkens the edges of objects a little instead"}.

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 26.0 sentence}}}The paper offers both versions, and so does the lab's dock.

### Where to read it {#c6-sources kind=2d sources="bias-source, eq12, weight-meaning, eq19, fig7, eq20, naive-m, eq22, talbot-cost, alg6, approx-pdf, unbiased-cost"}

Bias and its removal are in section four, and the example is figure seven.

## Experiments on this machine {#c7 number=7 target=84 summary="The paper's comparisons, run on this machine as the video is recorded." sources="results-setup, biased-vs-unbiased, sample-count"}

### The comparisons {#c7-methods kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoMethods panelPerformance {append baseRoom {append roomCamera {noReuse 32.0}}}}}
${cue "key" floatRoom}

With every part in place, the paper compares its methods.

These are the experimentation data run on this current machine, the one that recorded this video.

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 24.0 sentence}}}The panel on the right shows each pass's cost on this computer, as these frames render.

${cue "key" {' {callout "results-setup"}}}First comes streaming RIS alone, the baseline the paper compares against.

${overText {' {restirSpatialPassesRange 0.0 2.0}} "Next comes spatial reuse alone, which the paper offers for still images that have no previous frame"}.

${overText {' {restirTemporalRange 0.0 1.0}} "Then comes full spatiotemporal reuse, in its biased form"}.

The lab keeps one reservoir per pixel, where the paper's biased results keep four.

${cue "key" {' {glide cameraYaw 30.0 sentence}}}Watch how much noise disappears, and how little time the new passes take.

${cue "key" {' {callout "biased-vs-unbiased"}}}${overText {' {restirUnbiasedRange 0.0 1.0}} "Last comes the unbiased form, with its extra shadow rays in the spatial pass"}.

The paper reports that the biased form has less noise, and that the unbiased form loses no energy to bias.

### Against a reference {#c7-reference kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoReference panelHidden {append baseRoom {append roomCamera {' {restirAccumulate 1.0} {restirTemporal 0.0} {restirSpatialPasses 0.0}}}}}}

The reference is the room averaged over many frames of streaming RIS, which converges to the image every method estimates.

The package includes an experiment that measures each method's error against this reference, on whichever machine runs it.

### Where to read it {#c7-sources kind=2d sources="results-setup, biased-vs-unbiased, sample-count"}

The paper's own results are in section six.

## Conclusion {#c8 number=8 target=90 summary="The frame once more, the paper's conclusion and limits, three takeaways, and the references." sources="conclusion, limitations, screen-space"}

### The frame once more {#c8-recap kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoRoom panelHidden {append baseRoom {append roomCamera {noReuse 1.0}}}}}
${cue "key" floatRoom}

To close, here is the frame once more, in order.

${overText {' {restirCandidatesRange 1.0 32.0}} "Each pixel draws thirty-two candidates and keeps one, in a reservoir"}.

${overText {' {restirStageRange 0.0 1.0}} "A shadow ray tests the sample it kept"}.

${overText {' {restirTemporalRange 0.0 1.0}} "The previous frame then adds its reservoir"}.

${overText {' {restirSpatialPassesRange 0.0 2.0}} "Then the neighbours' reservoirs join in, round by round"}.

${cue "key" {' {restirStage 3.0}}}Only then is the pixel shaded.

### What the paper concludes {#c8-paper kind=2d diagram=alg5 focus="RIS, VIS, TEMP, SPAT, SHADE, PREV"}

${cue "key" {' {callout "conclusion"}}}The authors conclude that denoising need not be a separate step after rendering.

ReSTIR filters the chances of picking each light, rather than the colours.

${cue "key" {' {callout "limitations"}}}Its gain shrinks where neighbouring pixels differ, so their samples help each other less.

That happens where hidden surfaces come into view, at sharp changes in lighting, and with fast-moving lights.

${cue "key" {' {callout "screen-space"}}}It also works only at the first surface each pixel sees, and extending it to surfaces beyond is left for future work.

### Three things to remember {#c8-takeaways kind=3d}

${cue "key" {opening demoRoom panelHidden {append baseRoom roomCamera}}}
${cue "key" floatRoom}

${overText {' {lightPhaseRange 0.0 0.08}} "First, a reservoir summarises a whole stream of candidates in a few numbers"}.

${overText {' {lightPhaseRange 0.08 0.16}} "Second, reuse multiplies the candidates behind each pixel for the cost of only a few shadow rays"}.

${overText {' {lightPhaseRange 0.16 0.24}} "Third, reuse stays unbiased only when every source that could have produced a sample is counted"}.

### Where to read it {#c8-sources kind=2d sources="conclusion, limitations, screen-space"}

The conclusion and its limits are section eight.

